### [08_Jen Linkova_UnicastUnsolicitedRAs 02](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/126/materials/slides-126-v6ops-10-jenlinkova-unicastunsolicitedras-01)
* **Presenter**: Jen Linkova
* **Details**:
  * Multicast Router Advertisements (RAs) are frequently dropped over wireless networks. This is especially disruptive during renumbering events when life-time values are being aggressively decremented.
  * Jen proposed sending unsolicited RAs as unicast (using either L3 unicast to known link-local neighbors or L2 unicast) to increase reliability.
* **Discussion**:
  * Andriy Yurchenko noted that sending L3 unicast RAs might trigger Neighbor Uniqueness Detection (NUD) and agreed that L2 unicast of L3 multicast is a cleaner approach.
  * Ondřej Caletka agreed that sending unicast packets during network renumbering is an acceptable overhead to ensure delivery.
  * Tim Winters cautioned that home gateways typically decouple the RA generation process from the ND cache, making neighbor-table lookups difficult to implement.
  * Lorenzo Colitti argued that the best long-term solution is to lobby IEEE 802.11 to mandate L2 unicast conversion for critical multicast control packets (like RAs) at the Access Point.

